\input{sections/tab_main}

We ask whether the guarantee holds (\S\ref{sec:main}), what a tolerance buys (\S\ref{sec:tradeoff}), what
limits the savings (\S\ref{sec:monotone}), and which ingredients make certification efficient
(\S\ref{sec:testchoice}).

\subsection{The guarantee holds}
\label{sec:main}

Proposition~\ref{prop:risk} bounds the probability, over the calibration sample, that the deployed
policy loses more than $\eps$. We check this event directly by treating each recorded pool as the
task distribution, drawing calibration sets i.i.d.\ from it and computing the deployed policy's
exact risk: over 5{,}000 draws per setting and tolerance the failure rate is at most 3.4\% (upper
95\% limit 3.9\%) against $\delta = 10\%$ (Appendix~\ref{app:checks}). The finite-pool violation rate
of the replay agrees: at most 2\% in Table~\ref{tab:main} and 3\% anywhere in the tolerance sweep.

The guarantee is what distinguishes these savings from those of simple rules. Without it, rules of
similar cost lose an amount that can be large and is hard to predict. Always stopping at $L_0$ loses up to
20.6 points, more than 5 on ten of twelve settings; a fixed threshold on $L_0$'s confidence that spends
as much as \climb{} (62\% of the pipeline's tokens on average) loses 0.3 to 15.9 points, more than 2 on
seven settings and more than 5 on four, three of them Gemma's settings with inverted confidence, where the
threshold keeps exactly the confident repetitions; and the cheapest threshold that stays within 5 points
on one model loses up to 17 points on another (Figure~\ref{fig:motivation}d). Always stopping at the
single agent $L_1$, at 20 to 96\% of the pipeline's tokens, loses more than 2 points on eight settings and
up to 9.4 on Gemma's BBEH, with nothing at deployment to say where.

\subsection{What a tolerance buys}
\label{sec:tradeoff}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_tradeoff.pdf}
\caption{(a) Tokens as the tolerance $\eps$ varies (100 splits per point; violation rates at most
3\%). (b) Tokens as the calibration set grows ($\eps = 5\%$ and $10\%$).}
\label{fig:tradeoff}
\end{figure}

The team improves on the direct answer on every setting, by 2.0 to 20.6 points, so savings
are paid for in accuracy, and $\eps$ sets the price. At $\eps = 2\%$ \climb{} saves little, at most 16\%
of the pipeline's tokens on MuSiQue and nothing on MATH (Table~\ref{tab:main}). At 5\% it spends 31 to
96\% of the pipeline's tokens, 62\% on average, for losses of at most 2.8 points; at 10\% it spends 18 to
62\%, 35\% on average. Savings are smallest where the direct answer's confidence is inverted (Gemma on
FRAMES and BBEH), so that the certified policy falls back to the team, and on the open-book settings,
where the cheap rungs read the same documents as the team and pay for them too.
Figure~\ref{fig:tradeoff}a fills in the curve: MuSiQue starts saving at 2 to 3\%, the other benchmarks
mostly from 3 to 4\%.

Savings also need data. With 50 calibration tasks \climb{} certifies almost nothing, with 100 only at
10\%, and savings at 5\% need a few hundred (Figure~\ref{fig:tradeoff}b). Recording them costs 1.5M to
79M tokens, repaid after 340 to 2{,}000 queries at $\eps = 10\%$ and 460 to 4{,}400 at 5\%, except on
Gemma's BBEH, where the small saving at 5\% takes 13{,}000
(Appendix~\ref{app:review}).

\subsection{What limits the savings}
\label{sec:monotone}

Two things decide how much \climb{} can save, and neither affects the guarantee. \emph{The signal.} By
Proposition~\ref{prop:threshold} a threshold is optimal when the gain from escalating falls with
confidence. On the nine settings where $L_0$'s confidence ranks answers correctly (AUROC 0.63 to 0.81) it
does on eight: pooled over them, the gain falls from 21.6 points for the least confident tenth of tasks to
5 to 7 for the most confident (Figure~\ref{fig:criterion} in Appendix~\ref{app:monotone}). On Gemma's FRAMES, MuSiQue and BBEH the
ranking is inverted (AUROC 0.22 to 0.34): greedy decoding without thinking falls into repetitions with
high log-probability, and the certified policy falls back to the team. \emph{The design.} Stopping on the
agreement of the two cheap rungs is cheaper where the direct answer is weak, a threshold on $L_0$ where it
is strong; choosing between them on calibration data, as \climb{} does, is cheapest on average among five
designs certified the same way, and a learned estimate of the signed gain does no better
(Appendix~\ref{sec:variants}); a random score keeps the guarantee but saves less at 5 points
(Appendix~\ref{app:ablations}). An oracle that knows the cheapest correct rung would beat the pipeline by
up to 12 points, so the headroom lies in better signals.

\subsection{What makes certification efficient}
\label{sec:testchoice}

\input{sections/tab_calib}

Table~\ref{tab:calib} replaces parts of the procedure while keeping it valid. \emph{The test must adapt to
the variance.} Most stopped tasks keep the reference's answer, so most losses are zero; mapped to $[0, 1]$
they sit at $1/2$, and the Hoeffding--Bentkus bound used by existing signed-risk methods
\citep{wynn2026corrupted}, which assumes the worst variance at that mean, certifies nothing cheaper than the pipeline on our settings.
\emph{The loss must keep its sign.} Clipping it to the harm indicator counts the answers stopping loses but
not those it saves, and certifies much less where the team often loses correct answers, for instance 82
against 44\% of the pipeline's tokens on gpt-oss-20b MuSiQue (Table~\ref{tab:calibfull}); only on MATH,
where it rarely does, does an exact binomial test of the clipped loss do
as well or better. Testing the ordered family with a Bonferroni correction instead of in sequence loses
most of the remaining savings.
